## What to check

Compare the formal `shows`/`obtains` clause (and any `fixes`/`assumes`) against the natural-language statement. Look specifically for:

- A dropped or added hypothesis (the formal version is easier or harder than intended)
- A conclusion that is weaker (proves less than asked) or stronger (claims more than asked, which is itself suspicious) than the original
- Wrong quantifier scope or order (`\<forall>`/`\<exists>` flipped or misplaced)
- A specialized/narrower case substituted for the general statement (e.g. a specific instance instead of an arbitrary one, or a narrower type class than the mathematical claim requires)
- Sign/direction errors (e.g. `<` vs `\<le>`, an inequality reversed)
- A theorem that shares vocabulary with the statement but isn't actually what was asked

Ignore: variable/lemma naming, which tactics were used, proof style, and which libraries were imported (as long as the definitions used match their standard mathematical meaning).
